  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nSurface high pressure is centered nearly directly over central IL \nand will very slowly meander eastward over the next few days. This \nwill bring mostly clear skies and light winds. The air mass over the \nregion will very gradually moderate as well, with temperatures \nincreasing a few degrees each day.\n\nThe warming trend will continue midweek as southerly flow develops \nbehind the departing high as a low moves through the northern Plains \ninto the upper Midwest. A cold front trailing this system looks to \nstall out just northwest of central IL Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning and brings with it a slight chance of precipitation as\nfar southeast as the Illinois River. Otherwise, the warming \ntrend and dry conditions should continue through Friday. \n\nBy the weekend, large model discrepancies are shown by model\nensembles. There is a general theme of a stronger upper low \nprogressing across the central U.S., but timing and track varies\nsignificantly. While various scenarios could bring little or no\nrainfall to central IL, a blend of model solutions results in \n30-60 percent chances for precipitation between Friday night and\nSunday morning. Ensembles depict moderate chances (30-60%) for \na combined 100+ J/kg CAPE and 30+ kt deep layer shear with this \nsystem for the southern and central Plains, but only around 10 \npercent as far east as the IL River. Will need to keep an eye on\nthe exact solution for potential inclusion of thunderstorms, \nbut at this time chances appear too low this far east.\nProbabilities for storm total rainfall over an inch are only\n5-15 percent across central and southeast IL, so meaningful\ndrought reduction is unlikely. A push of colder air will likely\narrive starting Sunday behind this system. \n\nHighs should reach the lower to mid 50s today, mid 50s Monday, and \nupper 50s Tuesday. The warming trend will likely continue through \nSaturday, with highs from 65 to 70 forecast Saturday. Lows tonight \nshould be in the upper 20s, followed by upper 20s to lower 30s \nMonday night.",
